WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.779
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:10.210
We have had quite a few episodes on simulating
the motion of fluids and creating beautiful

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.099
footages from the results.

00:00:12.099 --> 00:00:17.380
In this work, the authors created a simulator,
that shows us not only the motion of a piece

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.700
of fluid, but the physics of bubbles within
as well.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:26.400
This sounds great, but there are two huge
problems: one, there are a lot of them, and

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.420
two, they can undergo all kinds of deformations
and topology changes.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:37.360
To conjure up video footage that is realistic,
and relates to the real world, several bubble-related

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:45.329
effects, such as entrainment, splitting, merging,
advection and collapsing, all have to be simulated

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:46.329
faithfully.

00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:51.691
However, there is a large body of research
out there to simulate bubbles, and here, we

00:00:51.691 --> 00:00:56.410
are not only interested in the footage of
this piece of fluid, but also what kind of

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:59.760
sounds it would emit when we interact with
it.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:34.210
The result is something like this.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:39.650
The vibrations of a bubble is simulated by
borrowing the equations that govern the movement

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.550
of springs in physics.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:48.630
However, this, by itself would only be a forlorn
attempt at creating a faithful sound simulation,

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:51.660
as there are other important factors to take
into consideration.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.970
For instance, the position of the bubble matters
a great deal.

00:01:55.970 --> 00:02:02.180
This example shows that the pitch of the sound
is expected to be lower near solid walls,

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:07.410
as you can see it marked with dark blue on
the left, right side and below, and have a

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:11.590
higher pitch near the surface, which is marked
with red.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:16.090
You can also see that there are significant
differences in the frequencies depending on

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:20.250
the position, the highest frequency being
twice as high as the lowest.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.880
So this is definitely an important part of
the simulation.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:29.840
Furthermore, taking into consideration the
shape of the bubbles is also of utmost importance.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.280
As the shape of the bubble goes from an ellipsoid
to something close to a sphere, the emitted

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.720
sound frequency can drop by as much as 30%.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:45.560
Beyond these effects, there were still blind
spots even in state of the art simulations.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:50.510
With previous techniques, a chirp-like sound
was missing, which is now possible to simulate

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.510
with a novel frequency extension model.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:58.750
Additional extensions include a technique
that models the phenomenon of the bubbles

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:00.652
popping at the surface.

00:03:00.652 --> 00:03:06.150
The paper discusses what cases are likely
to emphasize which of these extension's effects.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:41.270
Putting it all together, it sounds magnificent,
check it out!

00:03:41.270 --> 00:04:10.500
But still, however great these sounds are,
without proper validation, these are still

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.570
just numbers on a paper.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:17.840
And of course, as always, the best way of
testing these kinds of works if we let reality

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:21.810
be our judge, and compare the results to real
world footage.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:30.500
So I think you can guess what the next test
is going to be about!

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:41.830
The 
authors also put up a clinic on physics and

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:46.080
math, and the entirety of the paper is absolutely
beautifully written.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.700
I definitely recommend having a look, as always,
the link is available in the description box.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:57.600
Also, you'll find one more link to a playlist
with all of our previous episodes on fluid

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:58.600
simulations.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:04:59.800
Lots of goodies there.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.741
As always, we'd love to read your feedback
on this episode, let us know whether you have

00:05:03.741 --> 00:05:04.890
found it understandable!

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:06.370
I hope you did.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:10.280
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

